  { id:'opmcq-c18-1', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:"A 45-year-old man presents months after a metallic penetrating eye injury. He now has slowly worsening vision. Examination shows a brownish discolouration of the iris, a sluggish pupil, and rusty staining of the anterior lens capsule. ERG shows reduced amplitudes. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
    options:["Chalcosis","Siderosis","Uveitis","Traumatic cataract"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Siderosis (siderosis bulbi).** A retained *iron* intraocular foreign body slowly dissociates; the ferrous ions are taken up by epithelial tissues — lens epithelium, iris dilator, ciliary epithelium, retinal pigment epithelium — and poison them. `L25) Trauma I.txt` slides 46–47 name exactly this pairing: **Iron → siderosis, Copper → chalcosis, and inert bodies (stone, glass, plastic, wood)** which may be left alone.\n\nEvery element of the stem is a siderosis sign, and the combination is what makes the answer unambiguous rather than merely likely:\n\n| Finding in the stem | Tissue poisoned |\n| --- | --- |\n| Brownish (rusty) iris — heterochromia of the injured eye | Iris stroma and pigment epithelium |\n| Sluggish, poorly reacting pupil | Iris **dilator** muscle |\n| Rusty staining of the **anterior lens capsule** | Lens epithelium (which sits under the anterior capsule only) |\n| Reduced ERG amplitudes | Retinal pigment epithelium and photoreceptors |\n| Onset **months** after the injury | Slow ionic dissociation, not the injury itself |\n\n*(The individual signs above — the heterochromia, the rusty anterior-capsule deposits and the falling ERG amplitudes — are standard clinical description and are **not taken from the course material**, which names siderosis but does not list its signs. `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera.txt` mentions siderosis only in passing, as a cause of heterochromia it does not cover.)*\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| Chalcosis | ✗ The **copper** counterpart. It gives a Kayser–Fleischer-like ring, a sunflower cataract and a golden retinal sheen — not a rusty capsule, and it does not classically flatten the ERG the way siderosis does. |\n| **Siderosis** | ✅ Iron. Rusty iris, dilator paralysis, rusty anterior capsule, falling ERG, months after a metallic penetrating injury. |\n| Uveitis | ✗ Would be painful, red and photophobic with cells and flare, and would not stain the lens capsule rusty or reduce the ERG. |\n| Traumatic cataract | ✗ Explains blurred vision, but nothing else here — not the iris colour, not the pupil, and certainly not the ERG, which is a retinal test. |\n\n⚠️ The clue that separates this from a simple traumatic cataract is the **ERG**: it is a test of the retina, so an abnormal ERG places the disease behind the lens. Siderosis is retinopathy plus everything anterior; a cataract is the lens alone.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Recognise siderosis bulbi from a retained iron intraocular foreign body — rusty iris, sluggish pupil, rusty anterior lens capsule and a reduced ERG months after a metallic penetrating injury — and separate it from chalcosis (copper).",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.112 (Ch.18 Q1, the first question of the chapter, printed directly beneath the red banner "18. Ocular trauma")' },

  { id:'opmcq-c18-2', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:"Which of the following is the definitive treatment of an intraocular foreign body (IOFB)?",
    options:["Removal","Topical antibiotics","Observation","Eye patching"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Removal.** An intraocular foreign body is by definition an open-globe injury, and nothing that is left inside the eye stops causing harm. `L25) Trauma I.txt` slide 45 is explicit that an open globe — rupture or full-thickness laceration — **is repaired with sutures**, and slides 46–47 divide retained bodies into the two that must come out (iron → siderosis, copper → chalcosis) and the inert ones (stone, glass, plastic, wood). Grade Gain's `opqb-t18-718` puts the same rule the other way round: a small intraocular iron foreign body **must be removed**.\n\nThe other three are all things that are genuinely done for an IOFB — but none of them is *definitive*, and that is the word the question turns on.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Removal** | ✅ Surgical extraction with closure of the wound. It is the only step that ends the disease rather than holding it. |\n| Topical antibiotics | ✗ Prophylaxis, and a weak route at that — topical drops penetrate an intact cornea poorly, which is why systemic cover is used instead (see `opmcq-c18-26`). Adjunctive, never definitive. |\n| Observation | ✗ Reserved at most for a small, truly inert, well-tolerated body in a closed eye. It treats nothing, and with iron or copper it guarantees siderosis or chalcosis (`opmcq-c18-1`). |\n| Eye patching | ✗ ⚠️ Actively wrong here. `L25` slide 51 says place a **rigid eye shield** and **do not place a patch** — a patch presses on an open globe and can extrude its contents. |\n\nWork-up before removal, per `L25` slide 52: **plain X-ray, CT scan, ultrasound may be useful — and do not do an MRI if a metallic IOFB is suspected** (`opmcq-c18-17`).\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"State that removal is the definitive treatment of an intraocular foreign body, and rank antibiotics, observation and patching as adjunctive, inadequate or contraindicated.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.112 (Ch.18 Q2)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c18-3', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:"Which of the following conditions is associated with a deep anterior chamber?",
    options:["Scleral laceration","Corneal laceration","Intumescent traumatic cataract","A & B","B & C"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Scleral laceration.** The depth of the anterior chamber after trauma is a *localising* sign: it tells you roughly **where** the eye wall has opened, because aqueous and vitreous shift toward the hole.\n\n| Where the wall opens | What happens to the anterior chamber | Why |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Sclera** (posterior to the limbus) | **Deepens** | The leak is behind the iris–lens diaphragm, so the whole diaphragm falls backwards and the chamber gets deeper. |\n| **Cornea** (anterior) | **Shallows or flattens** | Aqueous escapes forwards through the wound and the iris comes up against the cornea. |\n| Intumescent (swollen) traumatic cataract | **Shallows** | The lens imbibes water, thickens, and pushes the iris forwards. |\n\n`L25) Trauma I.txt` slides 49–50 list among the signs that rule an open globe **in** a *shallow **or** deep anterior chamber* — the deck names both directions precisely because either one is abnormal and each points at a different site.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Scleral laceration** | ✅ A posterior leak lets the iris–lens diaphragm fall back: deep chamber. |\n| Corneal laceration | ✗ An anterior leak: shallow chamber. This is the key to the companion *shallow*-chamber question. |\n| Intumescent traumatic cataract | ✗ A swollen lens pushes forwards: shallow chamber. |\n| A & B | ✗ Corneal laceration shallows, so this pair cannot both deepen. |\n| B & C | ✗ Both members shallow the chamber — that combination is the answer to the *shallow* question, not this one. |\n\n⚠️ This question and its *shallow*-chamber companion are printed back to back with **the same five options in the same order**; only the words *deep* and *shallow* differ. Read the stem's adjective before the menu.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Use anterior chamber depth after trauma to localise the wound — a scleral (posterior) laceration deepens it; a corneal wound or a swollen lens shallows it.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.112 (Ch.18 Q3)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c18-4', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:"Which of the following traumatic conditions is associated with a shallow anterior chamber?",
    options:["Scleral laceration","Corneal laceration","Intumescent traumatic cataract","A & B","B & C"],
    answer:4,
    explanation:"**B & C — corneal laceration and intumescent traumatic cataract.** This is the mirror image of the *deep*-chamber question printed immediately before it, with an identical option list; the full deep-versus-shallow table is set out there and is not repeated here.\n\nBoth members of the key push the iris **forwards**, from opposite sides of the chamber:\n\n| Mechanism | Direction of the push |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Corneal laceration** | Aqueous drains out through the front of the eye; the chamber collapses and the iris rises to meet the cornea (it may even plug the wound — a *peaked pupil*, `L25) Trauma I.txt` slides 49–50). |\n| **Intumescent traumatic cataract** | The damaged lens takes up water and swells; the iris–lens diaphragm is shoved forwards from behind. |\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| Scleral laceration | ✗ Deepens the chamber — the leak is behind the diaphragm. |\n| Corneal laceration | ✗ True, but incomplete: it is one of the two. |\n| Intumescent traumatic cataract | ✗ True, but incomplete: it is the other one. |\n| A & B | ✗ Contains the scleral laceration, which deepens. |\n| **B & C** | ✅ The two anterior-pushing mechanisms, and the only pair in which both members shallow the chamber. |\n\n⚠️ An intumescent lens shallowing the chamber matters beyond localisation: it can close the angle and raise the pressure, which is why traumatic cataract is not simply watched.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Identify corneal laceration and intumescent traumatic cataract as the two traumatic causes of a shallow anterior chamber, and contrast them with the deepening produced by a scleral laceration.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.112 (Ch.18 Q4, the last question on the page; its option list is identical in text and order to Q3)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c18-5', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-orbit-mass',
    stem:"A 26-year-old male presents to the emergency department after severe head trauma. He complains of severe left eye pain with swelling of the left lower lid. Examination reveals limitation of ocular motility with ecchymosis and lid haematoma. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
    options:["Orbital floor fracture","Retrobulbar hemorrhage","Lid surgical emphysema","Hypopyon","None of the above"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Retrobulbar haemorrhage.** Blood collects in the closed retrobulbar space. The orbit is a bony box open only at the front, so the pressure inside it climbs until it strangles the eye — an **orbital compartment syndrome**, and one of the few true sight-threatening emergencies in ophthalmology. This entry carries the shared block for the case; `opmcq-c18-6`, `opmcq-c18-7` and `opmcq-c18-8` follow on from it and point back here.\n\n**The picture, and what the stem gives you**\n\n| Feature | In this stem? |\n| --- | --- |\n| Blunt/severe trauma | ✅ severe head trauma |\n| Severe pain | ✅ |\n| Lid ecchymosis and haematoma, tense swollen lids | ✅ |\n| Limitation of ocular motility (the globe is splinted by pressure) | ✅ |\n| Proptosis, falling vision, a relative afferent pupillary defect, a rock-hard orbit, raised IOP | ✗ not printed |\n\n**Grounding, stated plainly.** ⚠️ The cached lecture material does **not** describe this entity. A search for *canthotomy* and *cantholysis* across all 29 cached ophthalmology lecture files and all 12 cached book chapters returns exactly one hit, and it is `L25) Trauma I.txt`'s own note recording the omission. The book defers it in writing: `ophthalmology.pdf` p.58 lists \"Orbital/ Retrobulbar haemorrhage\" among the causes of exophthalmos and then says *(refer to ocular trauma chapter)*, giving only acute onset, a history of trauma or surgery, and \"ophthalmic emergency due to markedly raised IOP with risk of CRAO\" — and the book's own trauma chapter is not in the cache. The missing detail is supplied instead from **inside this module**: Grade Gain `opqb-t18-727` presents the same syndrome after a punch (proptosis, severe pain, reduced vision, tight lids, an RAPD) and keys lateral canthotomy with cantholysis, and `opqb-t18-728` keys the mechanism as *optic nerve compression and raised orbital pressure*.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| Orbital floor fracture | ✗ ⚠️ The closest distractor, and it genuinely fits ecchymosis, lid swelling and restricted motility (`L25` slides 19–24). It is separated by **mechanism of the motility defect** — a fracture *traps* the inferior rectus or inferior oblique, so diplopia is worst in **up- and down-gaze** and the eye tends to sink back (enophthalmos, infraorbital numbness). A retrobulbar bleed *pushes the eye forwards* and limits it in **every** direction. |\n| **Retrobulbar hemorrhage** | ✅ Severe pain, tense ecchymotic lids and globally limited motility after blunt orbital trauma. |\n| Lid surgical emphysema | ✗ Air, not blood — crepitus on palpation, typically after a medial wall fracture; painless and non-threatening. `L25` slide 20 lists subcutaneous emphysema as a *feature of* blow-out, not a diagnosis in its own right. |\n| Hypopyon | ✗ Pus layered in the anterior chamber — an intraocular sign of infection or severe inflammation, unrelated to lid ecchymosis and orbital motility. |\n| None of the above | ✗ B fits. |\n\n⚠️ **Divergence recorded, key not disputed.** The vignette as printed omits proptosis, visual loss, the RAPD and the tense orbit — the four findings that would make retrobulbar haemorrhage unarguable against option A. Both A and B fit the printed words. The key is B, and the three follow-up questions confirm the writer's intent: imaging withheld (`opmcq-c18-6`), a two-hour window (`opmcq-c18-7`) and canthotomy with cantholysis (`opmcq-c18-8`) are decompression answers, not fracture answers.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Diagnose retrobulbar haemorrhage as an orbital compartment syndrome after blunt orbital trauma, and separate it from an orbital floor fracture by the pattern of the motility defect.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.113 (Ch.18 Q5, the first question on the page and the antecedent case for Q6, Q7 and Q8)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c18-6', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-orbit-mass',
    stem:"A 26-year-old male presents to the emergency department after severe head trauma, with severe left eye pain, swelling of the left lower lid, limitation of ocular motility, ecchymosis and lid haematoma. What is the role of imaging in confirming the diagnosis?",
    options:["Imaging is mandatory before treatment","Imaging is not recommended as it's a clinically diagnosed condition and needs to be treated as soon as possible","The need for imaging depends on the situation and is determined by the ophthalmologist","Imaging is needed just to define the disease extent but not for diagnosis"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Imaging is not recommended — retrobulbar haemorrhage is a clinical diagnosis and the eye must be decompressed at once.** The case is the one set out in `opmcq-c18-5`; the stem here has been written out in full because the bank prints only \"In the previous case…\", which does not travel.\n\nThe reasoning is a time argument, not a diagnostic one. The retina tolerates ischaemia for a very short period (see the window in `opmcq-c18-7`), and a CT scan costs a large fraction of it — transfer, scan, report. Since the treatment is a bedside procedure requiring nothing that a scan could add, the scan can only subtract time from the retina. **A test that cannot change the next action but does delay it is a harmful test.**\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| Imaging is mandatory before treatment | ✗ Reverses the priority; the delay is the injury. |\n| **Imaging is not recommended as it's a clinically diagnosed condition and needs to be treated as soon as possible** | ✅ The diagnosis is made on the tense proptotic orbit, the pain and the falling vision; treatment does not wait for pictures. |\n| The need for imaging depends on the situation and is determined by the ophthalmologist | ✗ Too soft. This is one of the few ophthalmic situations with a single reflex answer, and the bank is testing that reflex. |\n| Imaging is needed just to define the disease extent but not for diagnosis | ✗ Half-right on the *diagnosis* half — imaging is indeed not diagnostic here — but it still puts a scan before the decompression. Extent is assessed afterwards, once the eye is safe. |\n\n*(The rule that imaging must not delay orbital decompression is standard emergency practice and is **not taken from the course material** — the cached lectures and book chapters do not describe retrobulbar haemorrhage at all; the measurement behind that statement is recorded in `opmcq-c18-5`.)*\n\n⚠️ Note the contrast with the neighbouring topic in this same chapter: for a **blow-out fracture**, imaging *is* the diagnostic step — `L25) Trauma I.txt` slide 23 and `ophthalmology.pdf` p.59 both call for a **coronal CT** and its tear-drop sign. The two orbital traumas take opposite approaches to the scanner, which is precisely why this question exists.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Recognise retrobulbar haemorrhage as a clinical diagnosis that must be treated before imaging, and contrast it with blow-out fracture, where coronal CT is diagnostic.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.113 (Ch.18 Q6, printed as "In the previous case…" — the previous case is Q5)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c18-7', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-orbit-mass',
    stem:"A 26-year-old male presents to the emergency department after severe head trauma, with severe left eye pain, a tense swollen lid and limitation of ocular motility from a retrobulbar haemorrhage. What is the time window during which the definitive treatment must be carried out?",
    options:["30 minutes","1 hour","2 hours","3 hours","4 hours"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**2 hours.** The case is `opmcq-c18-5`; the diagnosis is named in this stem because the question asks about the treatment of a condition already established, not about reaching it.\n\nThe number comes from retinal ischaemia. Raised orbital pressure closes the central retinal artery and stretches the optic nerve; beyond roughly **90–120 minutes** of complete retinal ischaemia the damage stops being reversible, so the orbit must be decompressed inside that window. This is the same clock that governs central retinal artery occlusion, and it is why `opmcq-c18-6` refuses to spend any of it on a CT scan.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| 30 minutes | ✗ Shorter than the accepted window. Sooner is always better, but this is not the taught figure and would make the condition effectively untreatable. |\n| 1 hour | ✗ Inside the window rather than the window itself. |\n| **2 hours** | ✅ The conventional limit for decompression before irreversible visual loss. |\n| 3 hours | ✗ Past it. |\n| 4 hours | ✗ Well past it; retinal damage is established. |\n\n*(The two-hour figure is standard emergency ophthalmology and is **not taken from the course material**. The measurement behind that statement is recorded in `opmcq-c18-5`: the entity is not described in any of the 29 cached lecture files, and the book chapter that would carry it is not cached. Grade Gain `opqb-t18-727` and `opqb-t18-728` supply the syndrome and its treatment from inside this module, but neither states a time limit — this figure has no in-module source at all.)*\n\n⚠️ The practical consequence of a two-hour window is that the treatment is a **bedside** procedure done by whoever is present (`opmcq-c18-8`), not a theatre case waiting for a list.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"State the approximately two-hour window for decompressing an orbital compartment syndrome, and connect it to the retinal ischaemia that makes the treatment a bedside procedure.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.113 (Ch.18 Q7, printed as "In the previous case…" — the previous case is Q5, not Q6)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c18-8', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-orbit-mass',
    stem:"A 26-year-old male presents to the emergency department after severe head trauma, with severe left eye pain, a tense swollen lid and limitation of ocular motility from a retrobulbar haemorrhage. What is the definitive treatment?",
    options:["Medical management","Lateral canthotomy & cantholysis","Lateral canthotomy, cantholysis & medical management","Watchful waiting","None of the above"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Lateral canthotomy, cantholysis *and* medical management.** The case is `opmcq-c18-5`.\n\nThe orbit is closed at the front by the orbital septum, which is anchored laterally by the lateral canthal tendon. Cutting that anchor is what opens the box:\n\n| Step | What it does |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Canthotomy** — a horizontal cut through the lateral canthus | Opens the angle and exposes the tendon. On its own it releases very little pressure. |\n| **Cantholysis** — dividing the inferior (and if needed superior) crus of the lateral canthal tendon | Releases the lower lid from its bony attachment, so the septum and lids swing forward and the globe is allowed to prolapse anteriorly. **This is the step that drops the pressure.** |\n| **Medical management** — intravenous acetazolamide and mannitol, topical pressure-lowering drops, steroids, head elevation | Lowers intraocular and orbital pressure while the surgical decompression is being prepared, and continues afterwards. |\n\n⚠️ Read the menu carefully: **option B is a strict subset of option C.** B is not wrong, it is incomplete, and the bank is testing whether you notice that the longer option contains the shorter one. Whenever two options stand in that relationship, the longer one is almost always the key.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| Medical management | ✗ Alone it cannot decompress a mechanically closed orbit — it buys minutes, not sight. |\n| Lateral canthotomy & cantholysis | ✗ The decisive manoeuvre, but the question asks for the *definitive treatment* and medical measures run alongside it. |\n| **Lateral canthotomy, cantholysis & medical management** | ✅ Surgical decompression plus pressure-lowering therapy. |\n| Watchful waiting | ✗ Guarantees the visual loss the two-hour window (`opmcq-c18-7`) exists to prevent. |\n| None of the above | ✗ C fits. |\n\n*(Canthotomy with cantholysis is grounded **inside this module** on Grade Gain `opqb-t18-727`, which keys it as the next immediate management of the same syndrome; the anatomical mechanism above and the specific medical agents are **not taken from the course material** — see the grounding note in `opmcq-c18-5`.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Name lateral canthotomy with cantholysis, combined with medical pressure-lowering therapy, as the definitive treatment of retrobulbar haemorrhage, and explain why cantholysis is the step that decompresses the orbit.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.113 (Ch.18 Q8, printed as "In the previous case…" — the previous case is Q5; the last question on the page before Q9\'s stem begins)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c18-9', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:"A 30-year-old man received a fist blow to his right eye during a quarrel with his wife, which caused severe eye pain. He went to an ophthalmologist, who examined the eye and found that the trauma had caused haemorrhage and accumulation of blood in the anterior chamber. The fluid level was sharply demarcated. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
    options:["Orbital floor fracture","Retrobulbar hemorrhage","Traumatic hyphema","Hypopyon","None of the above"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Traumatic hyphaema.** Blood in the anterior chamber after blunt trauma. This entry carries the shared hyphaema block for the chapter; `opmcq-c18-10`, `opmcq-c18-12`, `opmcq-c18-13` and the second hyphaema question in this chapter all point back here.\n\n**Why the layer settles.** Red cells are heavier than aqueous, so with the patient upright they sediment into the lower angle and form a **sharply demarcated horizontal fluid level** — the single most useful word in this stem. Blood that is merely dispersed through the aqueous is a *microhyphaema*; a level means a hyphaema.\n\n**Grading** (`L25) Trauma I.txt` slide 25) — by the fraction of the anterior chamber filled:\n\n| Grade | Chamber filled |\n| --- | --- |\n| I | Less than one third |\n| II | One third to one half |\n| III | More than one half |\n| IV | Total (\"eight-ball\" or blackball hyphaema) |\n\n**Complications** (`L25` slide 27) — **re-bleeding, commonest in the first 5 days**; uncontrolled high intraocular pressure causing optic nerve damage; **blood staining of the cornea**; central retinal artery occlusion. Set out in full at `opmcq-c18-12`.\n\n**Treatment** (`L25` slide 28) — hospitalisation for selected cases only, bed rest at 45°, cycloplegics (atropine), topical steroid, antiglaucoma agents if the pressure rises, and surgical evacuation for persistently high pressure or a total hyphaema lasting more than 5 days. Set out in full at `opmcq-c18-13`.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| Orbital floor fracture | ✗ A wall injury, not an intraocular one: diplopia on up- and down-gaze, enophthalmos, infraorbital numbness (`L25` slides 19–24). It puts no blood in the anterior chamber. |\n| Retrobulbar hemorrhage | ✗ Blood **behind** the globe, not inside it — proptosis and a tense orbit (`opmcq-c18-5`). The stem places the blood in the anterior chamber. |\n| **Traumatic hyphema** | ✅ Blunt fist injury, blood in the anterior chamber, a sharply demarcated level. |\n| Hypopyon | ✗ ⚠️ The layer-forming impostor. Hypopyon is layered **pus**, not blood — white or cream, and a sign of infection or severe inflammation. `L25`'s own teaching is that a hypopyon appearing after trauma means **endophthalmitis** (Grade Gain `opqb-t18-735`). The colour, and the fist, decide it. |\n| None of the above | ✗ C fits. |\n\n⚠️ **Printed as \"blooda accumulation\" and \"sharply demarkated\"** in the source; both are typographical slips in the book and are repaired in the stem above without change of meaning. The staging record `house-c18.array.js` keeps the printed forms.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Diagnose traumatic hyphaema from blunt injury with a sharply demarcated blood level in the anterior chamber, know its grading, and separate it from hypopyon and from retrobulbar haemorrhage.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf pp.113–114 (Ch.18 Q9; the stem begins at the foot of p.113, breaks mid-sentence and resumes at the top of p.114, where its options and key are printed. Antecedent case for Q10, Q12 and Q13)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c18-10', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:"A 30-year-old man received a fist blow to his right eye, causing traumatic hyphaema with a sharply demarcated blood level in the anterior chamber. What is the source of the bleeding?",
    options:["Iris root","Ciliary body","Choroid","Conjunctival vessels","Vitreous"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**The iris root.** The case is the fist-blow hyphaema case in this chapter.\n\nA blunt blow compresses the globe along its front-to-back axis, so it must expand at the equator. That sudden equatorial stretch tears the tissues at the **angle**, and the vessels that run there — the circulation at the iris root and the anterior face of the ciliary body — bleed into the anterior chamber. The same mechanism explains the associated blunt-trauma injuries: angle recession, iridodialysis (separation of the iris root, which gives **uniocular diplopia**, `L25) Trauma I.txt` slide 29) and cyclodialysis.\n\n*(The equatorial-expansion mechanism and the specific vessels — the major arterial circle of the iris and its branches — are standard anatomy and are **not taken from the course material**, which teaches hyphaema's grading, complications and treatment but does not name the bleeding vessel. The answer itself is grounded inside this module on Grade Gain `opqb-t18-729`, which asks the same question of a football injury and also keys the iris root.)*\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Iris root** | ✅ Where the iris meets the angle — the site torn by equatorial expansion, and the classical source. |\n| Ciliary body | ✗ ⚠️ The nearest miss. Its anterior face is part of the same injured zone and can contribute, but the standard single answer is the iris root; a ciliary body tear (cyclodialysis) more typically causes **hypotony**, not a hyphaema. |\n| Choroid | ✗ Posterior. Choroidal bleeding goes into the suprachoroidal or subretinal space, not forwards into the anterior chamber. |\n| Conjunctival vessels | ✗ Outside the eye entirely — these produce a **subconjunctival haemorrhage**, which lies on the surface and has no fluid level (contrast the second hyphaema question in this chapter). |\n| Vitreous | ✗ Behind the lens. Vitreous haemorrhage darkens the red reflex and blurs vision without layering in front of the iris. |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Name the iris root as the source of bleeding in traumatic hyphaema, and explain it by the equatorial expansion that a blunt anteroposterior blow forces on the globe.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.114 (Ch.18 Q10, printed as "In the previous case…" — the previous case is Q9. The book prints no question 11: the number is skipped and Q12 follows directly)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c18-12', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:"A 30-year-old man received a fist blow to his right eye, causing traumatic hyphaema with a sharply demarcated blood level in the anterior chamber. Which of the following is an expected complication?",
    options:["Blood staining of the cornea","Ischemic optic neuropathy","Rebleeding","All of the above","None of the above"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**All of the above.** The case is the fist-blow hyphaema case in this chapter. `L25) Trauma I.txt` slide 27 lists the complications of hyphaema as **re-bleeding, uncontrolled high intraocular pressure causing optic nerve damage, blood staining of the cornea, and central retinal artery occlusion** — three of which are printed here as options A to C.\n\n| Complication | Mechanism and timing |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Re-bleeding** | The commonest, and the one that governs management. It occurs as the original clot retracts and lyses — `L25` slide 27 places the risk in the **first 5 days**. A secondary bleed is usually larger than the first and is what turns a mild hyphaema into a blinding one. |\n| **Raised IOP → optic nerve damage** | Red cells, clot and inflammatory debris block the trabecular meshwork. The pressure rise can be acute and severe, and if it exceeds the perfusion pressure it also causes the **central retinal artery occlusion** on the deck's list. |\n| **Blood staining of the cornea** | Haemoglobin and its breakdown products are driven into the corneal stroma when a large or total hyphaema sits against a damaged endothelium at high pressure. It leaves a rusty-brown disc that clears over months to years, and in a child it can cause amblyopia. |\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| Blood staining of the cornea | ✗ True, but one of three. |\n| Ischemic optic neuropathy | ✗ True in substance, but one of three. |\n| Rebleeding | ✗ True, and the most important — still one of three. |\n| **All of the above** | ✅ Every listed item is a recognised complication. |\n| None of the above | ✗ All three are listed by the lecture. |\n\n⚠️ **Divergence recorded, key not disputed.** Option B is printed as \"ischemic optic neuropathy\". `L25` slide 27 words this complication as **uncontrolled high IOP causing optic nerve damage** — pressure-induced damage, which is not the same entity as anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy. The mechanism is nonetheless ischaemic (perfusion pressure falls as IOP rises), the printed key is D, and D stands.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"List re-bleeding, pressure-induced optic nerve damage and corneal blood staining as the complications of traumatic hyphaema, and place the re-bleeding risk in the first five days.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.114 (Ch.18 Q12, printed as "In the previous case…" — the previous case is Q9, not Q10. It is the first question printed after the skipped number 11)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c18-13', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:"A 30-year-old man received a fist blow to his right eye, causing traumatic hyphaema with a sharply demarcated blood level in the anterior chamber. Which of the following is the most appropriate line of management?",
    options:["Hospitalization","Elevation of the head of the bed","Cycloplegic eye drops","Surgical evacuation of blood in anterior chamber","All of the above"],
    answer:4,
    explanation:"**All of the above.** The case is the fist-blow hyphaema case in this chapter. Each option is a real component of hyphaema management drawn from `L25) Trauma I.txt` slide 28, and each has its own indication:\n\n| Measure | What it is for | The lecture's own qualification |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Hospitalisation** | Observation for the re-bleed of days 1–5, and for pressure control | ⚠️ **\"NOT EVERY CASE\"** — admission is for children, sickle cell disease, uncontrolled high IOP, and coagulation or bleeding disorders |\n| **Elevation of the head of the bed** | Bed rest **at 45°** so the blood sediments inferiorly, keeping the visual axis and the endothelium clear | Printed as bed rest at 45 degrees |\n| **Cycloplegic eye drops** | Atropine: rests the iris, relieves the pain of ciliary spasm, and prevents pupillary movement from disturbing the clot | Printed as \"cycloplegics (atropine)\" |\n| **Surgical evacuation** | Removing clot that the eye cannot clear | For **persistent elevated IOP, or a total hyphaema lasting more than 5 days** |\n\nAlso on the same slide but not offered as options: **topical steroid**, and **antiglaucoma treatment if the IOP is raised**.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| Hospitalization | ✗ A component, not the whole line. |\n| Elevation of the head of the bed | ✗ A component. |\n| Cycloplegic eye drops | ✗ A component. |\n| Surgical evacuation of blood in the anterior chamber | ✗ A component, and the one with the narrowest indication. |\n| **All of the above** | ✅ The question asks for the *line* of management, i.e. the whole protocol rather than a single next step. |\n\n⚠️ **Divergence recorded, key not disputed.** Read as \"do all four in every case\", this key contradicts the lecture twice: `L25` slide 28 says hospitalisation is **not** for every case, and restricts surgical evacuation to persistent high pressure or a total hyphaema beyond 5 days. Read as \"all four belong to the management of hyphaema\", the key is exactly right — and that is the reading the stem's phrase *line of management* supports. The printed key is E and E stands.\n\n⚠️ Do not confuse this with Grade Gain `opqb-t18-753`, which asks the same topic as an **EXCEPT** question and excludes bed rest in the **supine** position — supine is wrong precisely because it lets the blood spread across the visual axis instead of settling.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Assemble the management of traumatic hyphaema — selective admission, bed rest at 45°, cycloplegia, steroid, pressure control and evacuation when indicated — and state the lecture's limits on admission and surgery.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.114 (Ch.18 Q13, printed as "In the previous case…" — the previous case is Q9)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c18-14', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:"A 44-year-old female patient suffered severe eye trauma that led to a large subconjunctival haemorrhage. Digitally measured IOP was lower than normal. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
    options:["Globe rupture","Retrobulbar hemorrhage","Hyphema","Iridodialysis"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Globe rupture.** Two findings decide it, and both point the same way.\n\n**A large subconjunctival haemorrhage.** `L25) Trauma I.txt` slide 49–50 lists the signs that rule an open globe **in**, and one of them is a **bullous subconjunctival haemorrhage** — blood so extensive and elevated that it may be hiding a scleral wound underneath it. A trivial subconjunctival bleed is common and harmless; a large bullous one after severe trauma is a warning.\n\n**A low intraocular pressure.** Hypotony after trauma means the eye is leaking. An intact globe holds its pressure; a ruptured one cannot.\n\nThe other open-globe signs from the same slides, none of which the stem needed to give you: a **peaked pupil** (iris plugging the wound), a **shallow or deep anterior chamber** (the two anterior-chamber-depth questions earlier in this chapter), and a **positive Seidel test**. The vocabulary that separates rupture from laceration, penetration and perforation is set out once in the open-globe vocabulary entry later in this chapter.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Globe rupture** | ✅ Bullous subconjunctival haemorrhage plus hypotony after severe blunt trauma. |\n| Retrobulbar hemorrhage | ✗ Raises the pressure, it does not lower it — a tense proptotic orbit (`opmcq-c18-5`). The direction of the IOP change excludes it outright. |\n| Hyphema | ✗ Blood **inside** the anterior chamber with a level (the fist-blow hyphaema case in this chapter), not under the conjunctiva; and it more often raises the IOP than lowers it. |\n| Iridodialysis | ✗ Separation of the iris root from the ciliary body, giving a D-shaped pupil and **uniocular diplopia** (`L25` slide 29). It neither produces a bullous subconjunctival haemorrhage nor drops the pressure. |\n\n⚠️ **The stem contains a practice error worth naming, and it does not change the key.** The IOP here is **digitally measured** — palpated through the lid. `L25` slide 51 gives the rule for a suspected open globe: place a **rigid eye shield, do not place a patch, do not check the eye pressure**, and keep the patient nil by mouth for theatre. Pressing on a ruptured globe can extrude uveal tissue, vitreous or retina. The finding as printed is diagnostically decisive and clinically forbidden, and the printed key A stands. *(The book prints \"Digitally- measured\", with a space after the hyphen.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Diagnose globe rupture from a large (bullous) subconjunctival haemorrhage with hypotony after severe trauma, and recall the rule against palpating the pressure of a suspected open globe.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf pp.114–115 (Ch.18 Q14; stem and options A–B are printed at the foot of p.114, options C–D and the key at the top of p.115)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c18-15', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:"What is the grade of chemical eye injury that is characterised by total loss of the corneal epithelium and stromal haze obscuring iris details?",
    options:["Grade 1","Grade 2","Grade 3","Grade 4"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Grade 3.** This entry carries the shared chemical-burn table for the chapter; the chemical-burn prognosis question in this chapter, `opmcq-c18-21`, `opmcq-c18-23` and `opmcq-c18-24` point back here.\n\nGrading rests on **two** things and only two: how much **limbal ischaemia** there is, and how clear the **cornea** is (`L25) Trauma I.txt` slide 7; `L26) Trauma II.txt`). Limbal ischaemia is the prognostic half — the limbal stem cells are what re-surface the cornea, and once they are dead the eye cannot heal itself.\n\n| Grade | Limbal ischaemia | Cornea | Prognosis |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| I | None | Clear; epithelial damage only | Good / excellent |\n| II | Less than one third | Hazy, but **iris details visible** | Good |\n| **III** | One third to one half | **Total epithelial loss, stromal haze, iris details obscured** | **Guarded** |\n| IV | More than one half | Opaque; iris and pupil not seen | Poor / very poor |\n\nThe pivot between grade II and grade III is a single observation: **can you still see the iris through the cornea?** Visible details, grade II; obscured, grade III; cornea completely opaque, grade IV.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| Grade 1 | ✗ The cornea is clear and there is no ischaemia. |\n| Grade 2 | ✗ Hazy cornea, but iris details are still **visible** — the exact phrase the stem negates. This is the grade tested in the chemical-burn prognosis question in this chapter. |\n| **Grade 3** | ✅ Total epithelial loss with stromal haze obscuring the iris; one third to one half limbal ischaemia. |\n| Grade 4 | ✗ An **opaque** cornea, in which the iris is not merely obscured but invisible, with more than half the limbus ischaemic. |\n\n⚠️ Learn the wording, not the paraphrase. \"Obscured\" (grade III) and \"opaque\" (grade IV) are one word apart in the source and two grades apart in prognosis.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Grade chemical eye injuries on limbal ischaemia and corneal clarity, and identify total epithelial loss with stromal haze obscuring iris details as grade III.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.115 (Ch.18 Q15)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c18-16', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:"What is the prognosis of a chemical eye injury characterised by a hazy cornea with visible iris details?",
    options:["Excellent","Good","Guarded","Poor"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Good.** The described appearance — a hazy cornea through which the iris can still be seen — is **grade II**, and `L26) Trauma II.txt` gives grade II the prognosis **good**. The full grading table is set out once at the chemical-burn grading question in this chapter.\n\nThis question is a two-step: recognise the grade from the corneal description, then convert the grade into a prognosis.\n\n| Grade | Prognosis as printed in `L26) Trauma II.txt` |\n| --- | --- |\n| I | Excellent |\n| **II** | **Good** |\n| III | Guarded |\n| IV | Very poor |\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| Excellent | ✗ Grade I — a **clear** cornea with no limbal ischaemia. The stem's cornea is hazy, so it cannot be grade I. |\n| **Good** | ✅ Grade II: haze with iris details still visible, less than one third limbal ischaemia. |\n| Guarded | ✗ Grade III, where the haze **obscures** the iris (the chemical-burn grading question in this chapter). |\n| Poor | ✗ Grade IV, an opaque cornea with more than half the limbus ischaemic. |\n\n⚠️ Note that `L25) Trauma I.txt` slide 7 gives grade I and grade II the same word — \"good\" — while `L26) Trauma II.txt` separates them into \"excellent\" and \"good\". This menu offers both words, so it is `L26`'s four-word scale that the question is built on. Recorded because the two decks are not identical here.\n\nGrade Gain `opqb-t18-725` reaches the same grade from the other direction, giving limbal ischaemia below 30% with visible iris details and asking for the grade (Grade 2).\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Convert the corneal appearance of a chemical burn into its grade and then into its prognosis — hazy cornea with visible iris details is grade II, prognosis good.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.115 (Ch.18 Q16)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c18-17', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:"Which of the following imaging modalities is absolutely contraindicated in a patient with an intra-ocular metallic foreign body?",
    options:["CT","X ray","MRI","Ultrasound"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**MRI.** `L25) Trauma I.txt` slide 52 states the rule in capitals: **\"DO NOT DO MRI SCAN IF A METALLIC IOFB IS SUSPECTED.\"**\n\nThe reason is the magnet. An MRI scanner applies a powerful static magnetic field, and a ferromagnetic fragment inside the eye will move, rotate or heat in it — dragging through retina, choroid, lens or optic nerve and converting a removable foreign body into a blinding one. The contraindication is **absolute**, not relative: it is not a question of risk versus benefit, because the alternatives supply the same information safely.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| CT | ✗ Safe and the investigation of choice — `L25` slide 52 lists **CT scan** for localising an IOFB. |\n| X ray | ✗ Safe; the same slide lists **plain X-ray**, still useful for detecting and roughly locating a radio-opaque fragment. |\n| **MRI** | ✅ Absolutely contraindicated with a suspected metallic intraocular foreign body. |\n| Ultrasound | ✗ The slide notes **ultrasound may be useful**. ⚠️ It carries its own caution — pressing a probe on an eye that may be open is unwise (`opmcq-c18-14`) — but it is not the *absolute* contraindication the question asks for. |\n\nThe same rule appears twice in Grade Gain, once as an EXCEPT question about useful investigations (`opqb-t18-717`) and once by asking when MRI is contraindicated (`opqb-t18-739`) — a fact this course tests from every direction, which is a reliable signal that it is examinable.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"State that MRI is absolutely contraindicated when a metallic intraocular foreign body is suspected, and name X-ray, CT and ultrasound as the safe alternatives.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.115 (Ch.18 Q17)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c18-18', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:"Which of the following is a complication of intraocular foreign bodies?",
    options:["Endophthalmitis","Cataract","Retinal detachment","Vitreous hemorrhage","All of the above"],
    answer:4,
    explanation:"**All of the above.** An intraocular foreign body harms the eye in three separate ways, and the four options are drawn from all three.\n\n| Mechanism | Complications |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Mechanical** — the track the fragment tore on its way in | **Cataract** (any breach of the lens capsule), **vitreous haemorrhage**, **retinal detachment** (from a retinal tear or later traction) |\n| **Infective** — the fragment carries organisms through the wall | **Endophthalmitis**, the most feared early complication; a hypopyon appearing after trauma means endophthalmitis until proved otherwise (Grade Gain `opqb-t18-735`) |\n| **Toxic** — the metal itself dissolves over months | **Siderosis** from iron, **chalcosis** from copper (`L25) Trauma I.txt` slides 46–47, and `opmcq-c18-1`) |\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| Endophthalmitis | ✗ True — the infective route. One of four. |\n| Cataract | ✗ True — the fragment passing through the lens. One of four. |\n| Retinal detachment | ✗ True — a tear at the entry track, or later fibrous traction. One of four. |\n| Vitreous hemorrhage | ✗ True — vessels torn along the track. One of four. |\n| **All of the above** | ✅ Every listed item is a recognised complication of an IOFB. |\n\n*(The three-mechanism framework above is a teaching organisation of the material and is **not taken from the course material** as such; each individual complication is either taught in the ophthalmology lectures or is standard. It is set out here because it explains why an \"all of the above\" key is not a trick: the four options do not overlap.)*\n\n⚠️ This is why removal is definitive (`opmcq-c18-2`): only removal stops the toxic mechanism, and only wound closure stops the infective one.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"List the mechanical, infective and toxic complications of an intraocular foreign body — cataract, vitreous haemorrhage, retinal detachment, endophthalmitis, siderosis and chalcosis.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.115 (Ch.18 Q18)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c18-19', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:"What is the mechanism of brain damage in shaken baby syndrome?",
    options:["Trauma","Hypoxia from apnea caused by brainstem injury","Both A & B","Neither A nor B"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Hypoxia from apnoea caused by brainstem injury.**\n\n⚠️ **Grounding, stated first because it matters here.** Shaken baby syndrome appears **nowhere** in this module's cached material. Searching all 29 cached ophthalmology lecture files and all 12 cached book chapters for *shaken*, *shaking*, *non-accidental*, *nonaccidental* and *subdural* returns no relevant hit — the only \"shaking\" match is an unrelated line about a shaking environment, and the only \"abuse\" match is topical **anaesthetic** abuse in the cornea lecture. Neither trauma lecture mentions it. The whole of this answer is therefore **not taken from the course material**; it is filled from general paediatric and ophthalmic knowledge, and flagged as such.\n\n**Why the key is B and not A.** Violent shaking of an infant does cause direct mechanical injury — the head is heavy, the neck muscles are weak, and the bridging veins tear, producing the subdural haemorrhages and the multi-layered retinal haemorrhages that make the diagnosis. But the *direct* mechanical damage to brain tissue is usually modest. What produces the catastrophic, diffuse brain injury is the **cervicomedullary/brainstem injury**, which causes **apnoea**; the apnoea causes global **hypoxia–ischaemia**, and the hypoxic brain swells. Children who arrive apnoeic do far worse than those who do not. Hypoxia is the mechanism of the brain *damage*; trauma is the mechanism of the *event*.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| Trauma | ✗ The initiating event, and the cause of the subdural and retinal bleeding — but not the main mechanism of the parenchymal brain damage. |\n| **Hypoxia from apnea caused by brainstem injury** | ✅ Brainstem injury stops the baby breathing; the resulting hypoxic-ischaemic insult is what destroys the brain. |\n| Both A & B | ✗ ⚠️ The attractive answer, and the one the question is built to defeat. The bank has deliberately offered it and rejected it, which means it wants the *dominant* mechanism named rather than a list. |\n| Neither A nor B | ✗ B is correct. |\n\n**Why it is in an ophthalmology bank at all:** the ophthalmologist is often the one who makes the diagnosis. Multi-layered retinal haemorrhages extending to the periphery, out of proportion to any history given, are close to specific for the syndrome — so a dilated fundus examination is part of the child-protection work-up.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Name hypoxia from apnoea due to brainstem injury as the mechanism of brain damage in shaken baby syndrome, and recall the ophthalmologist's role in identifying its retinal haemorrhages.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf pp.115–116 (Ch.18 Q19; stem and option A are printed at the foot of p.115, options B–D and the key at the top of p.116)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c18-20', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-orbit-mass',
    stem:"A 26-year-old male patient comes to the emergency department after being injured in a quarrel. He reports that he received a fist blow. Which of the following is the most commonly fractured area in that kind of trauma?",
    options:["Medial orbital wall","Lateral orbital wall","Roof of the orbit","Floor of the orbit"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**The floor of the orbit.** This is the classical **blow-out fracture**. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.58 states that it is caused by an impacting object **greater in diameter than the orbital aperture** — a fist or a tennis ball — and that the fracture **most frequently involves the floor of the orbit**, sometimes the medial orbital wall. `L25) Trauma I.txt` slides 19–24 teach the same injury from the same history.\n\n**Why the floor.** The object cannot enter the orbit, so it transmits its force to the contents; orbital pressure rises sharply and the wall gives way where it is thinnest. The floor — the thin plate of maxilla over the maxillary sinus — is the weakest, and it collapses downwards into that air-filled space. The medial wall (lamina papyracea) is thinner still but is buttressed by the ethmoid air-cell septa, so it fractures second.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| Medial orbital wall | ✗ ⚠️ The nearest miss, and it is the **second** commonest — named by the book on the same page. Its own sign is **subcutaneous (surgical) emphysema** after nose-blowing, because it opens into the ethmoid sinus. |\n| Lateral orbital wall | ✗ The thickest and strongest wall (zygomatic bone and greater wing of sphenoid); it fractures only under great force. |\n| Roof of the orbit | ✗ Rare in adults; when it happens it is a neurosurgical problem, since above it is the frontal lobe. |\n| **Floor of the orbit** | ✅ The commonest blow-out site, collapsing into the maxillary sinus. |\n\n**What follows the fracture**, from `L25` slides 19–24 and `ophthalmology.pdf` p.59: ecchymosis and lid oedema, subconjunctival haemorrhage, **diplopia in both up- and down-gaze** from mechanical entrapment of the inferior rectus or inferior oblique, **infraorbital nerve anaesthesia**, and **enophthalmos**. Diagnosis is by **coronal CT**, which shows the classical **tear-drop sign** of orbital contents herniating into the sinus. The patient is told to **refrain from blowing the nose**, and systemic antibiotics are given.\n\n⚠️ Grade Gain asks this same anatomy but in the other unit: `opqb-t18-722` asks the most commonly fractured **bone** and keys the **maxillary**, which is the bone that forms this floor. Area versus bone — read which one is being asked. `opqb-t18-754` asks the movement most often defective and keys **elevation**.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Identify the orbital floor as the commonest site of a blow-out fracture after a fist injury, explain it by the thin maxillary plate over the maxillary sinus, and recall the fracture's signs and coronal CT diagnosis.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.116 (Ch.18 Q20)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c18-21', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:"Which of the following has the poorest prognosis?",
    options:["Injury to eye by sulphuric acid","Injury to eye by hydrochloric acid","Injury to eye by sodium hydroxide","All have similar effect and prognosis"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Injury by sodium hydroxide.** The menu is two acids and one alkali, and the whole question is whether you recognise which is which.\n\n| Agent | Class | Named in the lectures? |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sulphuric acid (H₂SO₄) | Acid | ✅ `L26) Trauma II.txt` |\n| Hydrochloric acid (HCl) | Acid | ✅ `L26) Trauma II.txt` |\n| **Sodium hydroxide (NaOH)** | **Alkali** | ✅ `L26) Trauma II.txt` lists NaOH first among the alkalis |\n\n**Alkalis are worse, and the reason is chemical.** `L25) Trauma I.txt` slide 4 and `L26` give it directly: alkalis **saponify cell membrane lipids and combine with stromal mucopolysaccharides**, so they dissolve their way through tissue and **penetrate deeply, continuing to cause damage for weeks**. Acids **coagulate surface proteins**, and the coagulum forms a **barrier that limits further penetration**. The acid injury is largely over when the exposure stops; the alkali injury is not.\n\nAlkali burns are also **twice as common** as acid burns (`L25` slide 2), because alkalis are what household and industrial cleaners, plaster, cement and lime are made of.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| Injury by sulphuric acid | ✗ An acid: self-limiting by protein coagulation. Serious, but not the worst here. |\n| Injury by hydrochloric acid | ✗ Also an acid, same reasoning. |\n| **Injury by sodium hydroxide** | ✅ The alkali. Deep, progressive, weeks-long damage and the worst prognosis. |\n| All have a similar effect and prognosis | ✗ Contradicts the central teaching point of the whole chemical-injury lecture. |\n\n⚠️ Do not read \"acids are mild\". **Concentrated sulphuric acid and hydrofluoric acid behave like alkalis** and penetrate deeply. The rule is a rule about the usual case, and the grading (the chemical-burn grading question in this chapter) still governs the individual prognosis.\n\nGrade Gain reaches the same fact directly: `opqb-t18-723` keys that alkalis penetrate deeper and continue for weeks.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Recognise sodium hydroxide as an alkali and explain why alkali burns carry the worst prognosis — saponification and deep, weeks-long penetration versus the protein coagulum that limits acid injury.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.116 (Ch.18 Q21; the options are printed with the British spellings "sulphuric" and "hydrochloric")' },

  { id:'opmcq-c18-22', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:"Which of the following is best described as two full-thickness wounds, one at the entry and one at the exit, usually caused by a missile?",
    options:["Open injury","Globe rupture","Laceration","Penetrating injury","Perforation"],
    answer:4,
    explanation:"**Perforation.** This entry carries the shared open-globe vocabulary table for the chapter; the companion definition question printed from this same five-option menu, the globe-rupture vignette `opmcq-c18-14`, and the two anterior-chamber-depth questions all draw on it.\n\nThe terms are a strict hierarchy, and the word that decides between the last two is **how many holes**:\n\n| Term | Definition | Mechanism |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Open globe injury** | Any **full-thickness** wound of the eye wall (cornea and/or sclera). The umbrella term — everything below is a kind of it. | `L25) Trauma I.txt` slide 45: rupture globe **or** full-thickness laceration; repaired with sutures |\n| **Rupture** | A full-thickness wound produced by a **blunt** object | The impact raises the intraocular pressure momentarily and the wall bursts — often at its weakest points, away from the site struck. `L25` slide 43: **rupture globe (blunt object)** |\n| **Laceration** | A full-thickness wound produced by a **sharp** object, cut at the point of contact | `L25` slide 44: partial- or full-thickness laceration, **sharp object** |\n| **Penetrating injury** | A laceration with an **entry wound only** — the object goes in and stays, or is withdrawn along its track | **One** hole |\n| **Perforation** | A laceration with **entry and exit wounds** — the object passes right through | **Two** holes; typically a high-velocity **missile** |\n| **IOFB** | A penetrating injury in which the object remains inside the eye | See `opmcq-c18-2`, `opmcq-c18-17`, `opmcq-c18-18` |\n\n*(The formal entry/exit definitions of penetration and perforation are standard trauma terminology and are **not taken from the course material**, which teaches rupture, laceration and open globe but does not separate penetrating from perforating injury. The rupture/laceration distinction above **is** from `L25` slides 43–45.)*\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| Open injury | ✗ The umbrella term. It is true of the described injury but does not distinguish it — every other option is also an open injury. |\n| Globe rupture | ✗ Blunt mechanism, and it does not produce a matched entry-and-exit pair. |\n| Laceration | ✗ The category to which perforation belongs, but a laceration by itself may have one hole or two. Not specific. |\n| Penetrating injury | ✗ ⚠️ The trap. Entry **only** — one wound. The stem says two. |\n| **Perforation** | ✅ Entry **and** exit; two full-thickness wounds; classically a missile. |\n\n⚠️ **This menu is printed twice in the chapter**, identically and in the same order, here and again later in the chapter. Only the definition inside the stem differs, and the two keys are different. Read the stem before the options.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Define the open-globe vocabulary — rupture, laceration, penetrating, perforating and IOFB — and identify a two-wound entry-and-exit missile injury as a perforation.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.116 (Ch.18 Q22; the definition is printed in parentheses inside the stem. Its five options are identical in text and order to those of Q25)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c18-23', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:"Grading of chemical eye injuries depends on which of the following factors?",
    options:["Corneal clarity","Duration of exposure","Severity of limbal ischemia","A & B","A & C"],
    answer:4,
    explanation:"**A & C — corneal clarity and the severity of limbal ischaemia.** These are the two axes of the grading table set out at the chemical-burn grading question in this chapter, and there are only two.\n\n| Axis | What is judged | Why it matters |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Corneal clarity** | Clear → hazy with visible iris → haze obscuring the iris → opaque | Measures how deep the injury reached, and how much vision it costs now |\n| **Severity of limbal ischaemia** | None → under one third → one third to a half → over a half | Measures whether the eye can still **heal**: the limbus holds the stem cells that re-surface the cornea, and a dead limbus means permanent conjunctivalisation and scarring |\n\n⚠️ **Option B is the trap, and it is a trap built out of something true.** `L25) Trauma I.txt` slide 3 lists what the **severity** of a chemical injury relates to: the properties of the chemical, the area of ocular surface affected, and the **duration of exposure**. Duration therefore genuinely determines how bad the injury *becomes* — but it is a fact from the **history**, not a finding at the **slit lamp**, and the grade is assigned from what the eye looks like when you examine it. A patient who cannot say how long the exposure lasted can still be graded; one whose cornea and limbus you cannot see cannot.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| Corneal clarity | ✗ One of the two axes, but incomplete alone. |\n| Duration of exposure | ✗ Determines severity (slide 3); it is not a grading axis. |\n| Severity of limbal ischemia | ✗ The other axis, and the prognostic one — still incomplete alone. |\n| A & B | ✗ Pairs a true axis with a historical factor and omits limbal ischaemia, the more important half. |\n| **A & C** | ✅ Corneal clarity plus limbal ischaemia: the two examination findings the grade is built from. |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"State that chemical injuries are graded on corneal clarity and limbal ischaemia, and distinguish those examination axes from historical determinants of severity such as duration of exposure.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.116 (Ch.18 Q23, the last question on the page)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c18-24', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:"What is the initial management in a case of chemical eye burn?",
    options:["Neutralization","Copious irrigation","Antibiotics","Eye patching"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Copious irrigation.** A chemical injury is **the most urgent ophthalmic emergency** (`L25) Trauma I.txt` slide 2) — it is the one condition treated *before* history, before visual acuity, before anything. The chemical keeps burning while it is in contact with the eye, so the whole of the initial management is getting it out.\n\n`L25` slides 9–10 and `L26) Trauma II.txt` give the method: **copious irrigation with saline, about 2 litres over 15–30 minutes, continued until the pH returns to normal**, with **eversion of the lids** to find and remove **retained particulate matter** — a step that is skipped at the eye's peril, since a fragment of lime or plaster hidden in the fornix goes on releasing alkali through any amount of irrigation.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| Neutralization | ✗ ⚠️ The trap, because it is not simply invented. `L26` does list antidotes — **boric acid 4%** for alkalis, **EDTA** for lime, **sodium bicarbonate 3%** for acids — but they come **after** irrigation, never instead of it. Neutralising an acid with a base on the ocular surface is an exothermic reaction: it adds a thermal injury to a chemical one. Dilution first, always. |\n| **Copious irrigation** | ✅ Immediate, prolonged, with lid eversion, until the pH normalises. |\n| Antibiotics | ✗ Part of subsequent care against secondary infection of an epithelial defect, not the initial step. |\n| Eye patching | ✗ Traps the chemical against the eye — the opposite of what is needed. |\n\n⚠️ **The single most examinable behaviour in this whole chapter: irrigate first, ask questions later.** Grade Gain `opqb-t18-716` puts the same rule as the first line of management of an acid injury — wash with water immediately — and `opqb-t18-734` keys chemical burn as the most urgent ophthalmic emergency.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Give copious immediate irrigation with lid eversion as the initial management of any chemical eye burn, and explain why neutralisation and patching are wrong first steps.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.117 (Ch.18 Q24, the first question on the page)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c18-25', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:"Which of the following is best described as a full-thickness defect in the eye wall produced by a tearing injury, usually as a result of a direct impact?",
    options:["Open injury","Globe rupture","Laceration","Penetrating injury","Perforation"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Laceration.** The five options are the open-globe vocabulary, set out in full in the companion definition question printed earlier in this chapter from this same five-option menu, and not repeated here. The discriminating phrase is **\"as a result of a direct impact\"** — the wound is made *where the object struck*, from the outside in. That is a laceration. A **rupture**, by contrast, is made from the **inside out**: a blunt blow raises the intraocular pressure for an instant and the wall bursts at its weakest point, which is often nowhere near where the eye was hit.\n\n| Term | Direction | Where the wound is |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Laceration** | Outside-in | **At the point of impact** |\n| Rupture | Inside-out | At the weakest point of the wall — behind the rectus insertions, at the limbus, or at an old surgical wound — often remote from the impact |\n\n⚠️ **Divergence recorded, key not disputed.** This stem is genuinely ambiguous against the module's own wording, and the ambiguity should be named rather than smoothed over:\n\n- `L25) Trauma I.txt` slide 43 assigns the blunt mechanism to **rupture globe (blunt object)**, and slide 44 assigns **laceration to a sharp object**.\n- Grade Gain `opqb-t18-750` asks for \"a full thickness wound of the eyewall caused by a **blunt** object\" and keys **Rupture**.\n- Read through those two sources, \"a tearing injury from a direct impact\" sounds like the blunt mechanism, i.e. option B.\n\nThe printed key is **C. Laceration**, and it is defensible: the stem says *direct impact* — at the site — and never says *blunt*, and a tear at the site of contact is by definition a laceration rather than a rupture. Note also that the bank prints this menu twice and needs two different keys from it (the other printing of this menu takes a different key), which constrains what this one can be. **The key stands as printed; the discrepancy is recorded, not corrected.**\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| Open injury | ✗ The umbrella term for any full-thickness wound; true but not discriminating. |\n| Globe rupture | ✗ ⚠️ The strongest challenger — see the divergence above. Excluded here because the stem locates the wound at the impact, not away from it. |\n| **Laceration** | ✅ A full-thickness tear of the eye wall at the point of contact. |\n| Penetrating injury | ✗ A subtype of laceration with an **entry wound only**; the stem describes the defect, not the object's track. |\n| Perforation | ✗ **Entry and exit** — two wounds. The key to the other question printed from this same menu. |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Classify a full-thickness eye wall defect produced at the site of a direct impact as a laceration, and contrast the outside-in laceration with the inside-out mechanism of a globe rupture.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.117 (Ch.18 Q25; the definition is printed in parentheses inside the stem, and its five options are identical in text and order to those of Q22)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c18-26', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:"What is the appropriate initial management when a penetrating eye injury is complicated by a suspected intraocular foreign body?",
    options:["Topical steroids","Oral antivirals","IV antibiotics","Warm compresses"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Intravenous antibiotics.** A penetrating injury with a retained foreign body has carried organisms directly into the vitreous, and the complication that must be prevented is **post-traumatic endophthalmitis** — an infection that can destroy an eye within days.\n\nThe route is the point of the question. Topical drops cannot reach the vitreous through an intact cornea in useful concentration, so prophylaxis is **systemic**, and given intravenously to get therapeutic levels quickly.\n\nThe rest of the initial package, from `L25) Trauma I.txt` slide 51: place a **rigid eye shield**, **do not patch**, **do not check the intraocular pressure**, keep the patient **nil by mouth** in preparation for theatre, give **tetanus prophylaxis**, and arrange imaging — **X-ray, CT, ultrasound if useful, and never MRI** (slide 52, `opmcq-c18-17`). The definitive step remains **removal** of the foreign body with closure of the wound (`opmcq-c18-2`).\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| Topical steroids | ✗ ⚠️ Actively dangerous as an initial step in a potentially contaminated open eye: steroid suppresses the response to infection. Steroids have a place later, once infection is excluded and the wound is closed. |\n| Oral antivirals | ✗ There is no viral disease here. Antivirals belong to herpetic keratitis, not to trauma. |\n| **IV antibiotics** | ✅ Systemic prophylaxis against endophthalmitis, at concentrations topical therapy cannot reach. |\n| Warm compresses | ✗ For a chalazion or a stye. Applying pressure and heat to an open globe is unhelpful and unsafe. |\n\n⚠️ Grade Gain `opqb-t18-737` lists intravenous antibiotics among the *appropriate* steps for an open globe while keying **measuring the IOP** as the thing that must not be done — the same protocol, tested from its other end.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Give intravenous antibiotics as prophylaxis against endophthalmitis in a penetrating injury with a suspected intraocular foreign body, and recall the shield-not-patch, no-IOP, nil-by-mouth, no-MRI package that surrounds it.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.117 (Ch.18 Q26)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c18-27', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:"A 20-year-old man presents after being punched in the eye during a fight. He reports decreased vision and eye pain. On examination, a visible fluid level of blood is seen in the anterior chamber. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
    options:["Subconjunctival hemorrhage","Hyphema","Vitreous hemorrhage","Corneal abrasion"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Hyphaema.** Blood with a **visible fluid level in the anterior chamber** after a blunt blow. The full account of hyphaema — its source, grading, complications and management — is set out once at the fist-blow hyphaema case in this chapter, which presents the same diagnosis in a different patient and carries three follow-up questions.\n\nThis question is about **where** the blood is. Each distractor puts it, or the injury, somewhere else:\n\n| Option | Where the problem is | Why it is not this |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Subconjunctival hemorrhage | **On** the eye, under the conjunctiva | Flat, bright red, painless, no effect on vision, and it can never form a fluid level — there is no fluid-filled space under the conjunctiva for blood to settle in. ⚠️ It matters for a different reason: a large *bullous* one after severe trauma suggests a globe rupture underneath (`opmcq-c18-14`). |\n| **Hyphema** | **In the anterior chamber** | ✅ Red cells sediment in the aqueous and form a sharply demarcated horizontal level; pain and blurred vision follow. |\n| Vitreous hemorrhage | **Behind the lens** | Also causes decreased vision after blunt trauma, but the blood is not visible as a level in front of the iris — it dulls or removes the red reflex and is seen on fundus examination or ultrasound. |\n| Corneal abrasion | **On the surface of the cornea** | Painful and blurring, with a fluorescein-staining epithelial defect — but no blood anywhere. |\n\n⚠️ **This is not a duplicate of the fist-blow hyphaema case in this chapter**, although both are blunt assaults keying hyphaema. The two vignettes differ in age, mechanism and findings, the option lists differ in length, membership and order, the correct option sits in a different position in each, and the fist-blow hyphaema case in this chapter is the antecedent for three follow-up questions while this one stands alone. The bank has deliberately opened and closed the chapter with the same lesson.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Recognise traumatic hyphaema from a visible blood level in the anterior chamber after a punch, and locate the distractors anatomically — subconjunctival, vitreous and corneal surface.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.117 (Ch.18 Q27, the last question of the chapter; nothing is printed below its answer line)' }
